#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba9f608-c7bc-5eb6-ae4e-5f62cb7222a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are Different"}] :question/id #uuid "975987da-103c-5f24-93f3-56991b9e8b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-14" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. What is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,-\\,4} f(x)$?\n\n![](images/ab/ab2abe13314c2005907dcd5d7077af8e87b7126474bbb0261f757d8ec7c00698.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^+} f(x)=\\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, the function $f(x)$ is not defined for $x<-4$, and consequently we cannot approach $x=-4$ from the left. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^-} f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4} f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e021f949-0f55-50b8-8681-0b1ce71b21d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are the Same"}] :question/id #uuid "a9e611ae-68f1-5618-a53b-2c9b3606d468" :question/math-academy-id "e-15" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,4} f(x)$.\n\n![](images/16/168433761f6629ad555a70acf5764f93246484c253f3c7ecba8fdfdfd87c2adf.png)" :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $4$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$. So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^-} f(x) = -\\infty \\,\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $4$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty$. So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^+} f(x) = -\\infty \\,\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4} f(x)=-\\infty \\,\n$$"}]}
